description: HQL 0.8.0 starts other programs and reads what they print as typed values, runs a program as a script, and reads a HyperMarkDown 0.1.0 vault. Running programs for each, what breaks, and what 0.9.0 brings.
---
# HQL 0.8.0: child processes, scripts, HyperMarkDown 0.1.0 { .hql-post-title }

<p class="hql-post-meta">24 November 2026 · Yauhen Yakimovich</p>

!!! abstract "TL;DR"

    - **Child processes.** `command("git", ["log"])` names a program. `source`
      reads what it prints as values, `sink` writes to it, and `run` gives its
      outcome as a `Result`. No shell reads the command line, and a non-zero
      exit status is a failure.
    - **Scripts.** A file that starts with `#!/usr/bin/env hql` runs as a
      command. It reads its arguments with `io.env.argv()`, and its result
      sets the exit status the shell reads.
    - **HyperMarkDown 0.1.0.** A vault's config reads `location_path`. In a
      document, only `hql#eval` and `hql#query` fences run; a plain `hql`
      fence is a code listing.
    - **Five changes break existing programs or projects.** Each has a
      one-line fix, listed under "What breaks".

HQL 0.8.0 is out. This post shows running programs for each of its three
features. Every program and every output below was run with this release.
[Install](/install/) has the binaries.

## A child process in a pipeline

A **child process** is another program that an HQL program starts. HQL
0.8.0 starts one with `command` from the module `io.process`, and reads
and writes it with the operations a file already has.

### Reading: `source`

This script counts the commits of each author in the repository it is run
in:

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *

source(command("git", ["log", "--format=%an"]), Lines)
| group(by = name => name)
| sort(by = g => g.key)
| map(g => {author: g.key, commits: g.items | size})
```

Run in a repository with three commits, two by Ada and one by Grace, it
prints:

```text
$ ./authors.hql
[{author: "Ada", commits: 2}, {author: "Grace", commits: 1}]
```

`command(…)` is a value and starts nothing. It is an endpoint, like a
file: `source` starts `git`, reads its standard output to the end, waits
for it to exit, and gives each line as a `String`. The rest is an ordinary
pipeline. `tap` reads the same output as a stream, one line at a time
while the child is still running.

### Writing: `sink`

`sink` writes one line to the child's standard input for each value, and
closes it when the values end. What the child prints goes to the program's
standard output:

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *

["The Hobbit", "Dune", "Frankenstein"] | sink(command("sort"), Lines)
```

```text
$ ./sorted.hql
Dune
Frankenstein
The Hobbit
```

The three lines were printed by `sort`. `target` does the same with its
whole input as one document.

### Handling a failure: `run`

`run` starts the child, waits for it, and gives the outcome as a `Result`:
`ok(Output)` when the child exits `0`, and `err(CommandFailure)` with a
`reason` otherwise. `git describe` exits `128` in a repository that has no
tag, and this script handles that case with a `match`:

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *

match command("git", ["describe", "--tags"]).run() {
    ok(out) => out.stdout | trim,
    err(e) given e.status == some(128) => "no tag yet",
    err(e) => fmt("git failed: {}", e.reason),
}
```

```text
$ ./tag.hql
"no tag yet"
$ git tag v1
$ ./tag.hql
"v1"
```

### Three rules

Three rules differ from what a shell script or a `subprocess` call does:

- **No shell reads the command line.** `git` receives its arguments exactly
  as written. No word is split and no `*` is expanded, so text from outside
  the program is always one argument and never a command.
- **A non-zero exit status is a failure.** Under `run` it is an `err`.
  Under `source`, `tap`, `sink` and `target` it stops the program. Run
  outside a repository, `authors.hql` stops, and the diagnostic gives the
  child's own error as the cause:

    ```text
    $ ./authors.hql
    fatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories): .git
    execute failure[io-failure]: command `git`: failed: exited, status 128
     --> ./authors.hql:4:1
      |
    4 | source(command("git", ["log", "--format=%an"]), Lines)
      | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
      = caused by: its standard error ended with:
                   fatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories): .git
    ```

- **A child does not outlive the program.** When a read stops early, a
  `timeout_ms` passes or `hql` is interrupted, `hql` terminates the child
  and every process the child started.

A project lists the programs its scripts may start with
`commands = ["git"]` in `.hql/config.toml`.

Child processes run on Linux, macOS and Windows. The book's page is
[Child processes](/latest/book/09-io/child-processes/).

## A script

A program in a file now runs as a command. The first line names `hql` as
the interpreter, and the words after the path are the program's arguments:

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
import io.env

type Book { title: String, status: String }

shelf = [
    Book(title = "Dune", status = "reading"),
    Book(title = "The Hobbit", status = "todo"),
    Book(title = "Leaves of Grass", status = "todo"),
]
wanted = env.argv()[0] | unwrap_or("todo")
shelf | filter(b => b.status == wanted) | map(b => b.title) | sink(stdout, Text)
```

```text
$ chmod +x todo.hql
$ ./todo.hql
The Hobbit
Leaves of Grass
$ ./todo.hql reading
Dune
```

`hql todo.hql reading` runs the same file, and `hql -c '<program>'` runs
program text. The exit status follows from the result: a failure, or a
result that is `err(e)`, exits `1`, and any other result exits `0`. The
book has the details: [Scripts](/latest/book/09-io/scripts/).

## A document vault under HyperMarkDown 0.1.0

HQL reads a folder of HyperMarkDown cards as a **vault**, and `hql render`
runs the queries a document holds. 0.8.0 follows
[HyperMarkDown](https://hypermarkdown.org/) 0.1.0 in two places.

A vault's `.hmd/config.toml` states where its cards start with
`location_path`:

```toml
location_path = "."
```

In a document, only a fence marked `hql#eval` or `hql#query` is a cell. A
plain `hql` fence is a code listing. This is `reading-list.hmd`, in a vault
with two book cards:

````markdown
# Reading list

```hql#query id=books
cards | filter(c => c.metadata.category == "book")
```

## Still to read

```hql#eval
books | filter(c => c.metadata.status == "todo") | map(c => c.title)
```

A listing is shown and not run:

```hql
books | size
```
````

````text
$ hql render reading-list.hmd
# Reading list

## Still to read

- The Hobbit

A listing is shown and not run:

```hql
books | size
```
````

The `hql#query` cell ran and wrote nothing. The `hql#eval` cell read its
value by `id` and wrote its output. The listing stayed as written. The book
has more: [Queries inside documents](/latest/book/24-documents/).

## What breaks

HQL is before 1.0, and 0.8.0 makes five breaking changes.

| What changed | What you see | The fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A vault config that sets `wiki` is refused | the vault is not loaded, and the message names both `wiki` and `location_path` | rename the key to `location_path` in `.hmd/config.toml` |
| A plain `hql` fence is a code listing | `hql render` no longer runs the fence or writes its output | mark each cell `hql#eval`, or `hql#query` for a cell that writes nothing |
| Every word after the program is the program's | `hql '<program>' --to json` passes `--to json` to the program | write options first: `hql --to json '<program>'` |
| A result that is `err(e)` exits `1` | a script whose last value is an `err` now fails in `&&` chains and CI | handle the `err`, with `match` or `unwrap_or`, where it is not a failure |
| `Path.join` keeps an absolute segment | `Path("/home/ada").join("/tmp")` is `/tmp`, not `/home/ada//tmp` | pass a relative segment to stay under the base |

Two smaller changes are in the same release. An `hql#query` cell writes no
output in any `--emit` mode. `--to text` writes a collection of strings as
one string per line. The
[changelog](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md) lists
every change.

## Next: 0.9.0

0.9.0 continues with processes. These are planned and not in 0.8.0:

- **Signals.** The program's own process id, the signals it receives, and
  sending a signal to a process.
- **A child's pipes wired to channels.** Reading a child's standard error
  as values, and feeding its standard input from a channel.
- **`parallel`.** Branches that run at the same time, for a conversation
  with a child: one branch writes questions while another reads answers.
- **Watchdogs.** A check that runs beside a child and terminates it when it
  stops being healthy.
- **Named pipes.** `fifo(name)` as an endpoint, on Unix and Windows.

Questions and bug reports are welcome on
[r/hqlang](https://www.reddit.com/r/hqlang/).
